**A token change carries through to designers' work.** I changed a core UNDRR spacing token from 30px to 32px in code and republished the library. In a pilot file built from 56 linked instances, a resource page at desktop and mobile widths, the layout updated and every edited title, toggled preset and instance override survived. Restoring the value returned the measured fields to their baseline.

**Designers don't need the plugin to use the library.** The pilot was composed entirely from the published library, with custom headings and local notes.

## What does a translation cost?

**Not a second story, but not free either.** The reviewed pull request added roughly 77,000 lines, about 33,000 of them construction recipes, test suites and audits. That is a real maintenance obligation sitting next to active product code.

**I chose a smaller ongoing commitment.** Automated sync stays for tokens and styles, which are cheap to keep current. Full structural regeneration is reserved for major versions and new components. The [code split is still pending](https://github.com/unisdr/undrr-mangrove/pull/1319#issuecomment-5993683966).

**Ownership has to be explicit.** Structure, Auto Layout constraints and brand modes belong to the system; copy, chosen presets and visibility toggles in an instance belong to the designer. Manual edits to the library itself still need a policy before the next rebuild, or the translation starts turning into a second story after all.

## Why did this work?

**The truth was defined before the translation started.** If the schemas and tokens had been vague, the plugin would have had nothing to triangulate against and the agents nothing to be checked against. The syndication work had already done that, for reasons that had nothing to do with Figma.

**Each discipline gets the same story in its own environment.** It is the same move as [putting analytics into editorial workflows](/work/2026/impact-story-undrr-analytics-overlay-mcp/): carry the domain rules to where another discipline already works, rather than asking them to come to you. Designers get Figma, developers get Storybook, and both are reading from the same structured content schemas and tokens.

